{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Replace six verbs with one noun and a coherent verb set.\n\n    rebar bridge setup | check-access | fsck | status | preview | sync | pause | resume\n\nThree verbs retire. `reconcile` goes with NO alias, because the defaults invert: `rebar reconcile`\nwith no arguments means dry-run (the CLI wrapper injects `--mode dry-run` at\n`_cli/__init__.py:145-160`, while `python -m rebar_reconciler` itself defaults to LIVE, and\n`_lib_ops.py:92` defaults to dry-run again — three entry points, two conventions). `bridge sync`\nwith no arguments mutates. Aliasing would be a silent behaviour change; anyone with `reconcile` in\na script must re-read the line.\n\n`bridge-status` retires because its producer has never existed in this repo — rebar was extracted\nfrom digital-service-orchestra on 2026-06-08 and the reader was a knowing tombstone from day one.\nNote the citation in its docstring, \"epic 3a03\", is DANGLING: in this repo 3a03 resolves to an\nunrelated test-isolation bug created 11 days AFTER the comment citing it. The real epic is\n`3006-e198-13db-4e1f`.\n\n`purge-bridge` retires: five commits, every one an automated lint sweep or safety retrofit, none a\nfeature or bug fix, and no script, workflow, or runbook invokes it. Its `--keep` flag is also\ninverted-sense (it deletes everything NOT matching).\n\n`--mode` disappears entirely. Its five values collapse to `preview`/`sync` plus `--max-changes N`;\nthe two `bootstrap-*` values carry no semantics beyond their cap integer (the only non-applier\nconsumers test `MODE_CAPS[m] != 0` and never read 10 or 100).\n\nFlags survive only if their help text can be written without internals. Ten candidates reduced to\nthree: `--max-changes N`, `--only <ids>`, `--except <ids>`, `--max-age <duration>`, plus the house\n`--output json`. Maintainer decision: `--only`/`--except` limit what is EXAMINED as well as what is\nchanged (unlike the internal `--filter-local-ids` they replace, which filtered only writes and was\nmarked \"for validation use only\" for that reason), and preview/sync remains the read/write knob.\n\n`check-access` is its own verb, not a flag on the health verb: all three narrowing-flag precedents\n(`flux check --pre`, `dbt debug --connection`, `gh auth status`) are READ-ONLY, and none mutates the\nremote — so the precedent does not transfer to an operation that creates and deletes a real Jira\nissue. `fsck` audits your records; `check-access` proves your access.\n\n`setup` calls `check-access` as its final validation step.\n\n## Review remediation (2026-08-06) — consumers, verb semantics, cap-0 split\n\n**B3 — The production consumer is enumerated and migrated.**\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:262` runs `rebar reconcile --mode \"$MODE\"`. This is not an\nincidental caller: `$MODE` is the documented ROLLBACK BRAKE (epic `3006-e198-13db-4e1f`), so retiring\n`--mode` removes an operational safety control unless its replacement is stated. Mapping:\n* `MODE=reconcile-check` / `MODE=dry-run` -> `bridge preview` (no mutation)\n* `MODE=bootstrap-strict` / `bootstrap-throttle` -> `bridge sync --max-changes 10` / `--max-changes 100`\n* `MODE=live` -> `bridge sync` (uncapped)\n* the brake's \"stop entirely\" position is no longer a mode at all — it is `bridge pause` (sibling\n  `1104`), which is strictly stronger because it is remote-persisted and applies fleet-wide rather\n  than to one workflow_dispatch invocation.\nThe `workflow_dispatch` input is renamed accordingly in the same change, so the brake is never absent.\n\n**B4 — The two cap-0 modes are behaviourally distinct, and both land in `bridge preview`.**\nThe collapse rationale covers the bootstrap-* cap integers but not the cap-0 pair, which are NOT the\nsame operation: `reconcile-check` takes NO lock, returns early at `__main__.py:577`, and emits a\nbound-pair discrepancy report; `dry-run` takes the lock, runs the full differ pass with\n`persist=False`, and writes a manifest. Neither output is derivable from the other. Sibling `424f`\nowns the merge and is explicit that this is real work, not a rename: ONE verb, ONE pass, two sections\n`{plan, drift}`, with `_compare_pair` DELETED and field detail sourced from the differ. This ticket\ntherefore retires the `--mode` spellings only; `424f` owns the behavioural merge, and `424f`\ndepends_on this ticket.\n\n**B6 — User-facing strings that name a retired verb are enumerated.**\nRetiring `bridge-status` leaves two strings pointing at a verb that no longer exists:\n`_engine_support/bridge.py:32` (`_USAGE = \"Usage: ticket bridge-status [--output json]\"`) and\n`src/rebar/schemas/bridge_status.schema.json:5` (whose description names\n`rebar bridge-status --output json`). Both are removed with the verb — the schema file is retired\noutright, since sibling `b69c` builds status on durable state and publishes its own schema.\n\n\n## Review remediation round 2 (2026-08-07)\n\n**B4 — `mode.py` is NOT deleted here; only the `--mode` flag is.**\nThe earlier AC listed `mode.py` alongside the flag, contradicting this ticket's own \"retires the\n`--mode` spellings only\" scope. Two live consumers keep `MODE_CAPS` and the `Mode` rank ladder alive\npast this ticket:\n* the **MCP surface** gates mutating runs on `MODE_CAPS[parsed] != 0` (`_mcp_reads.py:41,286,291`),\n  and that surface is retired by sibling `f789`, not here;\n* the **phase gate** ranks by `Mode` and — per sibling `1104`'s decision — its pause compatibility\n  value must remain a REAL `Mode` member (`gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"`), because a non-`Mode` shim\n  makes `_mode_blocks` swallow the `ValueError` and silently fail OPEN.\nSo `mode.py` survives with its table and ladder intact; its eventual disposition belongs to `f789`\nand `1104`. Deleting it here would break both.\n\n**B2 — Direct engine callers are enumerated and migrated.**\nFour call sites invoke the engine's `--mode` outside the CLI and break the moment the flag goes:\n`scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_validation_probe.sh:85,429` and\n`scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_field_validation_probe.sh:143,1787` (via their `run_reconciler`\nhelper), using `bootstrap-throttle` and `reconcile-check`. They migrate in the SAME change:\n`--mode bootstrap-throttle` -> `sync --max-changes 100`, `--mode reconcile-check` -> `preview`.\n\n**B3 — Engine `--mode` test consumers are enumerated.**\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_main.py` (14 occurrences, driving\n`main(['--mode=...'])`) plus `tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_support.py`,\n`test_reconcile_pass.py` and `test_user_guide_commands.py`. These MIGRATE rather than being deleted —\nthey are the regression cover for the entrypoint this ticket rewrites, so losing them would remove\nthe proof that the new surface behaves like the old one where it should.\n\n**B1 — Cross-verb functional assertions are SCOPED, because the edge cannot be added.**\nThe pause/resume/status behavioural chain needs state owned by `1104` (remote-persisted pause) and\n`b69c` (durable status). An edge from this ticket to either would be a CYCLE — both already\n`depends_on` this one, since they attach their verbs to the surface it creates. So this ticket proves\nonly what it alone owns; the behavioural assertions belong to the sibling that builds the state.\n\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `rebar bridge <verb>` exists with the agreed verb set\n- [ ] `reconcile`, `bridge-status`, `purge-bridge` are removed, with release notes; no aliases\n- [ ] The `--mode` FLAG is gone from the CLI and the engine entrypoint (`_cli/__init__.py`'s\n      reconcile wrapper, `rebar_reconciler/__main__.py`'s argparse). **`mode.py` SURVIVES this\n      ticket** — see B4 below\n- [ ] **Surface scope is explicit (review remediation).** `reconcile`/`mode` lives on THREE surfaces,\n      not one: the CLI, the public library API `rebar.reconcile(mode=...)` (`src/rebar/__init__.py:51`\n      re-exporting `_lib_ops.py:92`), and the MCP tool `reconcile(mode=...)` (`_mcp_reads.py:279`,\n      surfaced via `mcp_server.py`). THIS ticket retires the CLI + engine only. The library and MCP\n      retirements belong to sibling `f789`, which ADDS the bridge surface to those same two\n      interfaces — so each surface's old verb is removed by the same change that ships its\n      replacement, leaving no window where an MCP or library caller has neither. `f789` already\n      depends_on this ticket, which enforces that order.\n- [ ] A test asserts the CLI no longer accepts `--mode` or the `reconcile` verb, AND that the library\n      and MCP entrypoints are still callable at this ticket's boundary (proving the staged retirement\n      left no surface broken mid-epic)\n- [ ] Every shipped flag's help text contains no internals (no \"mode\", \"rank\", \"gate\", \"lock\", \"cap\", \"pass\", \"binding store\", no exit-code numbers)\n- [ ] `bridge` appears in `rebar --help`. Today `reconcile` appears ZERO times while FOUR\n      bridge-prefixed verbs are listed — `purge-bridge`, `bridge-status`, `bridge-fsck`,\n      `bridge-probe` — two of which (`purge-bridge`, `bridge-status`) are dead. Verified against\n      `rebar --help` on 2026-08-06\n- [ ] The dangling \"epic 3a03\" citation is corrected wherever it survives\n- [ ] Each verb this ticket OWNS is proven to function, not merely to exist: `preview` performs no\n      Jira write and no ticket-event write (asserted against a store snapshot before and after), and\n      `sync` mutates. `check-access` round-trips and `setup` calls it as its final step\n- [ ] `pause`, `resume` and `status` are proven only to EXIST and dispatch here; their behavioural\n      assertions belong to `1104` and `b69c` respectively, which own the underlying state and already\n      depend on this ticket — an edge back would be a cycle (B1)\n- [ ] `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:262` is migrated off `rebar reconcile --mode \"$MODE\"`,\n      and its `workflow_dispatch` mode input is renamed to the new vocabulary in the SAME change, so\n      the epic-3006 rollback brake is never absent (B3)\n- [ ] `_engine_support/bridge.py:32`'s `_USAGE` string and `schemas/bridge_status.schema.json` are\n      removed with the `bridge-status` verb; a test greps the shipped tree for the retired verb names\n      and fails if any user-facing string still names one (B6)\n- [ ] The health verb is `fsck` everywhere — `doctor` is not shipped, not as an alias, and appears\n      nowhere in help, docs or code (B2/B7)\n- [ ] `check-access` is in the shipped verb set and `setup` calls it as its final validation step\n- [ ] The four direct engine callers in `scripts/jira-pressure-test/` are migrated in the same change\n      (B2); a test or CI step proves no script invokes `--mode` after this ticket\n- [ ] The engine `--mode` tests are MIGRATED, not deleted (B3), and still cover the same entrypoint\n      behaviours under the new vocabulary\n- [ ] `mode.py`'s `MODE_CAPS` table and `Mode` ladder still exist and are still consumed at the end of\n      this ticket; a test asserts the phase gate can still round-trip `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"`\n      as a real `Mode` member (B4)"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786086407638686001,"uuid":"e39e42a2-82bb-488f-a1aa-64dcec6e88bc"}